Year = {2024},
Volume = {7},
Number = {4},
Pages = {1504-1525},
Month = {AUG},
Abstract = {In this article, we explore the extent to which applying embodied, `skin
   in the game' methods used in infrastructure activism (a contemporary art
   practice) can help expand the toolkit of methods applied in action
   research on infrastructures, interactions between humans and
   more-than-humans and urban socio-environmental processes in planning. In
   particular, we focus on two cases of infrastructure activism: the
   Amsterdam Zuidoost Food Forest (VBAZO), in Amsterdam's South East, and
   the KRATER project in Ljubljana's city centre. In our discussion of
   these projects, we explore the embodied research practices that
   infrastructure activists have developed to change not only urban green
   infrastructures but also researcher-actors' own perspectives.},
