   Baranauskaite, Agne and Karamon, Jacek and Sroka, Jacek and Cencek,
   Tomasz},
Title = {Molecular Detection of Sarcocystis suihominis and Sarcocystis
   miescheriana in Wild Boars Hunted for Human Consumption in Poland
   Highlights a Potential Risk for the Consumer},
Journal = {ZOONOSES AND PUBLIC HEALTH},
Year = {2026},
Month = {2026 SEP 7},
Abstract = {Introduction: Wild boars and domestic pigs can act as intermediate hosts
   for two Sarcocystis species: Sarcocystis miescheriana and the zoonotic
   Sarcocystis suihominis. Humans become infected with S. suihominis by
   eating raw or undercooked meat. Herein we investigated the occurrence of
   Sarcocystis spp. in wild boars hunted for human consumption in Poland.
   Methods: Diaphragms were collected from 241 wild boars during two
   hunting seasons; a multiplex-PCR protocol targeting the mtDNA cox1 gene
   of S. miescheriana and S. suihominis was applied; diaphragm samples
   testing positive for S. suihominis underwent sarcocysts isolation and
   molecular characterization. Results: Out of 241 wild boars, 216 (90\%)
   revealed the presence of S. miescheriana DNA, including eight (3.3\%)
   co-infected with S. suihominis. The diaphragms of three wild boars
   testing positive for S. suihominis and S. miescheriana were further
   processed, yielding 37 sarcocysts. Two out of 37 isolated sarcocysts
   tested positive for S. suihominis and were further molecularly
   characterized, showing 98.9\%-100\% identity on the 18S rRNA gene and
   98.7\% identity on the cox1 mtDNA gene with S. suihominis. Thirteen of
   the remaining sarcocysts were molecularly characterized, leading to the
   identification of S. miescheriana in 12 cysts; one out of the 13
   sarcocysts yielded a 1053 bp cox1 mtDNA sequence and a 923 bp 18S rDNA
   sequence showing < 89.1\% and 95.6\% homology, respectively, with any
   known Sarcocystis species. Conclusions: This is the first study
   identifying Sarcocystis spp. circulating in wild boars intended for
   human consumption in Poland, revealing a non-negligible rate of
   infection with the zoonotic S. suihominis, a high rate of infection with
   S. miescheriana and the presence of a putative new Sarcocystis sp. Our
   study highlights that the risk for the consumers of undercooked wild
   boar meat cannot be excluded.},
